LIST:
stores elements in order and allows duplicates.
Duplicates are allowed.

1.ArrayList :
--> Stores data in dynamic array format.
--> Maintains insertion order
--> Allows duplicates
--> Fast for reading data

Syntax:
ArrayList<DataType> objectName = new ArrayList<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.ArrayList;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        ArrayList<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>(); //Creates an empty ArrayList named fruits
        fruits.add("Apple");
        fruits.add("Banana");
        fruits.add("Mango");
        System.out.println(fruits);
    }
}

2.LinkedList :
--> Stores elements using nodes connected together.
--> Fast insertion/deletion
--> Allows duplicates
--> Maintains order

Syntax :
LinkedList<DataType> objectName = new LinkedList<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.LinkedList;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        LinkedList<String> names = new LinkedList<>();
        names.add("Tejesh");
        names.add("Kusuma");
        names.add("Varshini");
        System.out.println(names);
    }
}

3.Vector :
--> It is similar to ArrayList but synchronized (thread-safe).
-->Maintains order
-->Allows duplicates
-->Slower than ArrayList

Syntax :
Vector<DataType> objectName = new Vector<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.Vector;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Vector<Integer> numbers = new Vector<>();

        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);

        System.out.println(numbers);
    }
}

SET:
Set does NOT allow duplicate values.

1.HashSet :
-->It stores unique elements.
-->No duplicates
-->Unordered

Syntax :
HashSet<DataType> objectName = new HashSet<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.HashSet;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
        set.add(10);
        set.add(20);
        set.add(10);
        System.out.println(set);
    }
}

Output:
[10, 20] //Duplicate 10 is ignored.

2.LinkedHashSet
-->Stores unique elements while maintaining insertion order.

Syntax :
LinkedHashSet<DataType> objectName = new LinkedHashSet<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.LinkedHashSet;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        LinkedHashSet<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        set.add("A");
        set.add("B");
        set.add("A");
        System.out.println(set);
    }
}

3.TreeSet :
-->Stores unique elements in sorted order.

Syntax :
TreeSet<DataType> objectName = new TreeSet<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.TreeSet;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        TreeSet<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
        set.add(50);
        set.add(10);
        set.add(30);
        System.out.println(set);
    }
}

Output:
[10, 30, 50] //Automatically sorts values.

QUEUE: It follows FIFO (First In First Out).

1.PriorityQueue
--> It Stores elements based on priority/sorted order.

Syntax :
PriorityQueue<DataType> objectName = new PriorityQueue<>();

Simple Code :

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.add(30);
        pq.add(10);
        pq.add(20);
        System.out.println(pq);
    }
}

Output :
[10, 30, 20] //Smallest element gets highest priority.

2.Queue using LinkedList :

Simple Code :

import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.LinkedList;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.add("A");
        queue.add("B");
        System.out.println(queue);
        queue.remove();
        System.out.println(queue);
    }
}

Output :
Before remove: [A, B]
After remove: [B]

MAP:
-->Stores data in key-value pairs.
101 -> Ram
102 -> Sam

1.HashMap:
-->Stores key-value pairs.
-->Keys unique
-->Unordered

Syntax:
HashMap<Key, Value> objectName = new HashMap<>();

Simple Code:

import java.util.HashMap;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put(1, "Ram");
        map.put(2, "Sam");
        System.out.println(map);
    }
}

Output :
{1=Ram, 2=Sam}

2.LinkedHashMap
Maintains insertion order.

Simple Code:

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        LinkedHashMap<Integer, String> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        map.put(3, "C");
        map.put(1, "A");
        System.out.println(map);
    }
}

✅ Output
{3=C, 1=A}

3.TreeMap :
Stores key-value pairs in sorted order of keys.

Simple Code:

import java.util.TreeMap;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        TreeMap<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
        map.put(3, "C");
        map.put(1, "A");
        System.out.println(map);
    }
}

Output:
{1=A, 3=C}

4.Hashtable:
-->Thread-safe version of HashMap.
-->Synchronized
-->No null key/value

Simple Code:

import java.util.Hashtable;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Hashtable<Integer, String> table = new Hashtable<>();
        table.put(1, "Java");
        table.put(2, "Python");
        System.out.println(table);
    }
}

Output:
{2=Python, 1=Java}

| Type          | Order   | Duplicates | Sorted         |
| ------------- | ------- | ---------- | -------------- |
| ArrayList     | Yes     | Yes        | No             |
| LinkedList    | Yes     | Yes        | No             |
| Vector        | Yes     | Yes        | No             |
| HashSet       | No      | No         | No             |
| LinkedHashSet | Yes     | No         | No             |
| TreeSet       | Yes     | No         | Yes            |
| PriorityQueue | Partial | Yes        | Priority Based |
| HashMap       | No      | Keys No    |                |
| LinkedHashMap | Yes     | Keys No    |                |
| TreeMap       | Sorted  | Keys No    |                |
| Hashtable     | No      | Keys No    |                |


